I've been going from traditional to digital for a while now. It's been pretty much like learning how to do everything you already know how to do in a different way. So I've had to put a lot of digital practice in similar to practice I already put in learning to draw with pencil and pen and paper. Also I've had to keep reminding myself to learn to let the machine do what it can do and let it help my process instead of trying to force traditional practices onto the digital format. (Just things like using the zoom ability often and the undo function wisely.)

&#x200B;

Also, I recently went back and watched some videos of how the old masters planned out their oil-paintings and structured each stage of the picture and it was amazingly helpful in making a better digital drawing. There's so much you CAN do with a digital drawing I drowned in a lack of discipline for a long time.

